{
  "headline": "Corn slips 4½¢ to $4.56 as crude climbs 3.7% to $103",
  "lead": "Corn closed lower at $4.56, while crude jumped 3.7% to $103. Soybeans edged up to $12.57½. Grain is soft and fuel is firm, so a farmer's input bill is rising while the corn check is not.",
  "teaser": "Corn eases to $4.56 while crude climbs 3.7% to $103.13 and natural gas gains 8.6%.",
  "one_number": {
    "value": "$28.1 billion",
    "unit": "combined crop loss at harvest, 2025",
    "context": "Farmdoc puts the 2025 crop loss at harvest at $28.1 billion across the nine cost-of-production crops. That is the margin backdrop behind today's quiet grain trade."
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Corn eases, beans edge up",
      "body": "- Corn is at **$4.56**, down 4½ cents, and soybeans are up 4½ cents to $12.57½.\n- Friday's market recap called it a fizzle: modest changes in corn, soybeans and winter wheat heading into the weekend.\n- Grain merchandisers are carrying larger inventories, high freight costs and margin call exposure, which can strain their working capital.",
      "so_what": "Harvest supply is still holding corn down while beans hold steady.",
      "conviction_level": "low",
      "overnight_surprise": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Cattle hold near $230",
      "body": "- Live cattle are at **$229.675**, about flat, and feeders are up 0.7% to $348.35.\n- USDA's October Cattle on Feed report showed September placements and marketings the lowest for the month in series history.\n- Beef Magazine says cash strength keeps driving cattle higher, though rising kill numbers raise questions.",
      "so_what": "Tight supply is holding up feeders even with live cattle flat.",
      "conviction_level": "medium",
      "overnight_surprise": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Crude and gas jump",
      "body": "- WTI crude is at **$103.13**, up 3.7% from Thursday's $99.47 settle, and natural gas is up 8.6% to $3.04.\n- Oilprice reports Brent nearing $120 as Saudi-Houthi tensions fuel another weekly rally.\n- Diesel and fertilizer costs tend to follow crude, so fall input bills are worth watching.",
      "so_what": "Higher crude adds to input costs while grain prices stay soft.",
      "conviction_level": "medium",
      "overnight_surprise": true
    }
  ],
